    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Track the farthest reachable index.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\nfunction canJump(nums: readonly number[]): boolean {\n  let farthest = 0;\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n    if (i > farthest) return false;\n\n    farthest = Math.max(farthest, i + nums[i]!);\n\n    if (farthest >= nums.length - 1) return true;\n  }\n\n  return nums.length === 0;\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN:\n```ts\nfunction canJumpUsingReduce(nums: readonly number[]): boolean {\n  let farthest = 0;\n\n  return nums.every((jump, index) => {\n    if (index > farthest) return false;\n\n    farthest = Math.max(farthest, index + jump);\n    return true;\n  }) && (nums.length === 0 || farthest >= nums.length - 1);\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — Example:\n[2,3,1,1,4]\ni=0 → farthest=2\ni=1 → farthest=4\ntarget reached → true.\n\n[3,2,1,0,4]\nfarthest becomes 3, but index 4 is unreachable → false.\n\nStep 3 — Complexity:\nO(n) time, O(1) auxiliary space.\n\nStep 4 — Key invariant:\nAt each index up to the current reachable boundary, `farthest` is the maximum position reachable from any processed index.",
