    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Sort arrivals and departures.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\nfunction minimumPlatforms(\n  arrivals: readonly number[],\n  departures: readonly number[],\n): number {\n  if (arrivals.length !== departures.length || arrivals.length === 0) {\n    return 0;\n  }\n\n  const a = [...arrivals].sort((x, y) => x - y);\n  const d = [...departures].sort((x, y) => x - y);\n\n  let i = 0;\n  let j = 0;\n  let current = 0;\n  let best = 0;\n\n  while (i < a.length) {\n    if (j === d.length || a[i]! <= d[j]!) {\n      current++;\n      best = Math.max(best, current);\n      i++;\n    } else {\n      current--;\n      j++;\n    }\n  }\n\n  return best;\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN:\n```ts\nfunction minimumPlatformsUsingSort(\n  arrivals: readonly number[],\n  departures: readonly number[],\n): number {\n  const a = [...arrivals].sort((x, y) => x - y);\n  const d = [...departures].sort((x, y) => x - y);\n\n  let i = 0;\n  let j = 0;\n  let current = 0;\n  let best = 0;\n\n  while (i < a.length) {\n    if (j >= d.length || a[i]! <= d[j]!) {\n      current++;\n      best = Math.max(best, current);\n      i++;\n    } else {\n      current--;\n      j++;\n    }\n  }\n\n  return best;\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — Example:\nArrivals [900,940,950,1100,1500,1800]\nDepartures [910,1200,1120,1130,1900,2000]\nmaximum overlap determines the required platforms.\n\nStep 3 — Complexity:\nO(n log n) due to sorting, O(n) additional memory for copies.\n\nStep 4 — Tie rule:\nIf a train arriving at the exact departure time still needs a separate platform, process arrival before departure (`<=`). If the platform can be reused immediately, process departure first (`<`).",
