    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Use a min-heap of frequencies.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\nfunction huffmanMergeCost(frequencies: readonly number[]): number {\n  const heap = [...frequencies].sort((a, b) => a - b);\n  let totalCost = 0;\n\n  while (heap.length > 1) {\n    const first = heap.shift()!;\n    const second = heap.shift()!;\n    const merged = first + second;\n\n    totalCost += merged;\n\n    let index = 0;\n    while (index < heap.length && heap[index]! < merged) {\n      index++;\n    }\n\n    heap.splice(index, 0, merged);\n  }\n\n  return totalCost;\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN:\n```ts\nfunction huffmanMergeCostUsingSort(\n  frequencies: readonly number[],\n): number {\n  const heap = [...frequencies].sort((a, b) => a - b);\n  let total = 0;\n\n  while (heap.length > 1) {\n    const a = heap.shift()!;\n    const b = heap.shift()!;\n    const merged = a + b;\n\n    total += merged;\n    heap.push(merged);\n    heap.sort((x, y) => x - y);\n  }\n\n  return total;\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — Example:\n[5,7,10,15,20,45]\nrepeatedly combine the two smallest.\nThe total merge cost is the weighted path-length objective used by Huffman coding.\n\nStep 3 — Complexity:\nThe conceptual greedy rule is O(n log n) with a real min-heap.\nThe simple array `shift + insert/sort` versions above are educational baselines and can be O(n² log n)-ish because array front operations and repeated sorting are expensive.\n\nStep 4 — Interview takeaway:\nThe greedy insight is \"combine the two least frequent items first\"; the data structure that makes this efficient is a min-heap.",
