    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Compare two classic problems.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE — greedy example:\n```ts\nfunction maxActivitiesByGreedy(\n  activities: readonly [number, number][],\n): number {\n  const sorted = [...activities].sort((a, b) => a[1] - b[1]);\n  let count = 0;\n  let lastEnd = -Infinity;\n\n  for (const [start, end] of sorted) {\n    if (start >= lastEnd) {\n      count++;\n      lastEnd = end;\n    }\n  }\n\n  return count;\n}\n```\n\nDP example:\n```ts\nfunction coinChangeDP(\n  coins: readonly number[],\n  amount: number,\n): number {\n  const dp = new Array<number>(amount + 1).fill(Infinity);\n  dp[0] = 0;\n\n  for (let value = 1; value <= amount; value++) {\n    for (const coin of coins) {\n      if (coin <= value) {\n        dp[value] = Math.min(\n          dp[value]!,\n          dp[value - coin]! + 1,\n        );\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  return dp[amount] === Infinity ? -1 : dp[amount]!;\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — Counterexample for greedy coin selection:\ncoins=[1,3,4], amount=6.\nGreedy picks 4+1+1 = 3 coins.\nDP finds 3+3 = 2 coins.\n\nStep 3 — Decision rule:\nUse greedy when a locally optimal choice can be proven safe for the whole problem.\nUse DP when choices interact and different partial solutions need to be compared/stored.\n\nStep 4 — Interview takeaway:\nDo not classify a problem by appearance. Prove the greedy choice or model the states.",
